//* -----------------------------------------------------------------
//* MVSMF -- STEPLIB concatenation for the HTTPD started task
//*
//* mvsMF is a CGI: httpd LINKs the MVSMF load module per request from
//* its own STEPLIB. SMP installs it into MVSMF.LINKLIB, so that
//* library has to be reachable from the HTTPD procedure.
//*
//* Edit the STEPLIB DD of your HTTPD procedure to read:
//* -----------------------------------------------------------------
//STEPLIB  DD  DISP=SHR,DSN=MVSMF.LINKLIB
//         DD  DISP=SHR,DSN=HTTPD.LINKLIB
//*
//* MVSMF.LINKLIB IS DELIBERATELY FIRST.
//*
//* If a copy of MVSMF was ever placed into HTTPD.LINKLIB by hand -- the
//* development deploy does exactly that -- then whichever library comes
//* first wins, and an SMP install into the other one activates nothing
//* while every step reports success. Putting the SMP-owned library in
//* front makes the installed level the one that runs. Better still,
//* delete the hand-placed copy; two copies of a load module are a
//* question nobody wants to answer during an incident.
//*
//* APF: EVERY LIBRARY IN AN AUTHORIZED STEPLIB CONCATENATION MUST BE
//* APF-AUTHORIZED. HTTPD.LINKLIB is, because httpd and mvsMF do
//* authorized work -- SVC 34 for operator commands, ACEE handling for
//* identity. Concatenating a library that is NOT authorized silently
//* de-authorizes the whole task, and the failure that follows does not
//* name the cause.
//*
//* So add MVSMF.LINKLIB to SYS1.PARMLIB(IEAAPF00) with the volume it
//* actually lives on:
//*
//*   MVSMF.LINKLIB WORK00,
//*
//* The volume is part of the entry, not decoration: an APF entry naming
//* a different volume than the allocation does not authorize anything.
//* MVS 3.8j reads IEAAPF00 at IPL and has no dynamic APF update, so this
//* change needs an IPL before the library is usable.
